WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.660
Purified neurons fire, yet barely talk to each other.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.500
What are they missing?

3
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:14.610
Ben Barres's laboratory purified rat retinal ganglion cells, leaving cultures with

4
00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:15.610
almost no glia.

5
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:22.210
In 1997, Frank Pfrieger and Barres reported that these neurons fired action

6
00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:23.210
potentials.

7
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:25.933
But they showed little synaptic activity.

8
00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:28.943
Astrocytes are not the only source.

9
00:00:30.003 --> 00:00:31.983
Neurons form some synapses without them.

10
00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:37.593
The readout is the mini: the small current from one vesicle released on its own.

11
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:41.553
Tetrodotoxin blocks action potentials.

12
00:00:41.773 --> 00:00:45.633
What remains depends on the synapses, not on how busy the neurons are.

13
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:51.127
With firing blocked, mini frequency is roughly the number of synapses giving

14
00:00:51.127 --> 00:00:54.447
detectable minis, times their average release rate.

15
00:00:55.227 --> 00:00:59.847
Mini size reflects the receptors facing the vesicle, and the transmitter inside it.

16
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.880
Now add a layer of glia that does not touch the neurons.

17
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.410
Mini frequency rose about twelvefold.

18
00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:09.510
Survival did not change.

19
00:01:10.483 --> 00:01:14.583
Predict. More synapses, or more release from each one?

20
00:01:20.917 --> 00:01:23.097
Mini frequency alone cannot say.

21
00:01:23.097 --> 00:01:28.837
More minis can mean more synapses, more release, or synapses that start responding.

22
00:01:29.817 --> 00:01:34.657
So Pfrieger and Barres also counted synapses by electron microscopy.

23
00:01:35.637 --> 00:01:38.237
Glia raised the count only about 2.3 times.

24
00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:44.210
If the same share of synapses gave detectable minis, each would release about five

25
00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:49.270
times as often. So the paper read most of the rise as more effective synapses.

26
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.680
In 2001, Erik Ullian and colleagues reported a sevenfold rise in synapses, in their

27
00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:03.200
own cultures. That is far more than the 1997 count, so how much is number and how

28
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.280
much is efficacy depends on the culture.

29
00:02:09.733 --> 00:02:16.213
In 2005, Karen Christopherson and colleagues identified thrombospondins as astrocyte

30
00:02:16.213 --> 00:02:17.893
proteins that induce synapses.

31
00:02:19.017 --> 00:02:22.377
Those synapses looked normal by electron microscopy.

32
00:02:23.177 --> 00:02:24.257
They released transmitter.

33
00:02:25.157 --> 00:02:27.577
Yet they gave no AMPA receptor responses.

34
00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:30.247
They were silent near rest.

35
00:02:31.147 --> 00:02:36.347
They lack working AMPA receptors, and any NMDA receptors there would be mostly

36
00:02:36.347 --> 00:02:37.447
blocked by magnesium.

37
00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:41.830
They add to the count, but add no AMPA minis.

38
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:46.970
Later work found glypicans 4 and 6, also from astrocytes.

39
00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.950
They recruit AMPA receptors, and the synapses respond.

40
00:02:55.433 --> 00:02:57.573
So astrocyte signals share the work.

41
00:02:58.433 --> 00:02:59.793
Thrombospondin mainly builds.

42
00:03:00.593 --> 00:03:02.573
The glypicans mainly switch synapses on.

43
00:03:03.533 --> 00:03:07.213
Takeaway: a synapse that is there is not yet a synapse that works.

44
00:03:08.213 --> 00:03:09.453
Count it, and record from it.
